---
/**
 * /solvers/precision/ — precision as a type parameter.
 *
 * Copied from `components/home/SolverStack.astro`'s precision block, which
 * stays on the landing page until cleanup retires it.
 *
 * The 11x figure is reported with its provenance rather than as a bare number.
 * It traces only to `corridor/README.md`, in a section that flags itself as
 * recorded on the superseded surrogate-era build, and the f64 baseline quoted
 * in the same table (about 35 s) disagrees with the committed artifact (44.4 s).
 * Both facts belong next to the ratio, or the ratio should not be published.
 */
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import SiteHeader from '../../components/nav/SiteHeader.astro';
import { Code } from 'astro:components';
import { rustDark, rustLight } from '../../../shiki-rust-themes.mjs';
import { MACHINE } from '../../consts';

const SHIKI = { light: rustLight, dark: rustDark };

const title = 'Precision as a parameter — DeepCausality CFD';
const description =
  'Every theory, solver, stage and observable is generic over one real scalar. Changing a single alias reruns the whole program at f32, f64 or 106-bit, which turns the error budget into a measurement.';

const alias = `/// Working precision.
pub type FloatType = f64; // or f32, or deep_causality_num::Float106`;

const lift = `/// Lift an exact \`f64\` specification into the working precision.
pub fn ft(x: f64) -> FloatType {
    FromPrimitive::from_f64(x).expect("specification lifts into FloatType")
}`;

/** The committed precision ladder from the Taylor-Green MMS target. */
const ladder = [
  { alias: 'f32', err: '3e-8', note: 'Fast, and the floor of the ladder.' },
  { alias: 'f64', err: '1e-16', note: 'The default, and the industry-standard choice.' },
  { alias: 'Float106', err: '8e-33', note: 'Reference-grade, up to 30 significant digits.' },
];
---
<BaseLayout title={title} description={description} path="/solvers/precision/">
  <SiteHeader slot="header" />

  <article class="page ppage">
    <header class="phead">
      <p class="eyebrow">Solvers · Precision</p>
      <h1>Precision as a parameter</h1>
      <p class="lede">
        One alias fixes the working precision for the whole computation, from
        the flux stencils through to the Kalman filter. That turns the error
        budget from an argument into an experiment.
      </p>
    </header>

    <section class="pgroup">
      <h2>One alias</h2>
      <p class="body">
        Every theory, solver, stage and observable is generic over a single real
        scalar. A program fixes the alias and the whole run follows it,
        downcasting to <code>f64</code> only at the display boundary.
      </p>
      <Code code={alias} lang="rust" themes={SHIKI} />
      <p class="body">
        Specification constants stay exact <code>f64</code> literals and are
        lifted into the working precision, so changing the alias reruns the same
        program somewhere else on the ladder rather than silently rounding its
        inputs first.
      </p>
      <Code code={lift} lang="rust" themes={SHIKI} />
    </section>

    <section class="pgroup">
      <h2>The measured ladder</h2>
      <p class="body">
        Taylor–Green through the incompressible Navier–Stokes RHS kernel with
        exact autodiff derivatives, against the closed form. Committed at
        <code>verification/mms_taylor_green_verification/</code>, on {MACHINE}.
      </p>
      <div class="table-scroll">
        <table>
          <thead>
            <tr>
              <th scope="col">Alias</th>
              <th scope="col">Max abs error</th>
              <th scope="col">Notes</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            {ladder.map((r) => (
              <tr>
                <th scope="row"><code>{r.alias}</code></th>
                <td class="num">{r.err}</td>
                <td>{r.note}</td>
              </tr>
            ))}
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="pgroup">
      <h2>Where it holds today</h2>
      <p class="body">
        The turbulence example is the clearest demonstration, because it runs
        all three precisions in one program and reports what each buys. The same
        rate field and the same <code>Rk4</code> march, instantiated at
        <code>f32</code>, <code>f64</code> and <code>Float106</code>, put the
        forecast horizon of a chaotic flow at t ≈ 21.5, 44.5 and about 81. Each
        sixteen digits roughly doubles the trustworthy lead time.
      </p>

      <div class="callout callout-warn">
        <p class="callout-title">The plasma-blackout corridor does not currently build at 106-bit</p>
        <p>
          It did once, and the result is worth stating: every gate and every
          discrete event step came out identical to the <code>f64</code> run,
          with the continuous witnesses agreeing to 15 or 16 significant digits,
          at roughly eleven times the wall clock. That was recorded on the
          superseded surrogate-era build and has no committed timing artifact.
        </p>
        <p>
          Re-flying it today fails to compile. The generic scalar reaches most of
          the way, but the corridor example has accumulated <code>f64</code>-specific
          code, and a handful of spots in the crate have too. So treat the 11×
          ratio and the identical-gates result as history rather than as a
          property you can reproduce, and take the working ladder from the
          turbulence example and the manufactured-solution target above.
        </p>
      </div>

      <p class="body">
        Precision is load-bearing at the bottom of the range as well as the top.
        The corridor at <code>f32</code> crashed at step 1, because
        <code>h²</code> in the then-flown Saha kernel (4.4e-67) underflows the
        <code>f32</code> exponent range, and the position ulp at Earth radius is
        about 0.5 m, which would swallow a sub-meter navigation result anyway.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="pgroup">
      <p class="more">
        <a href="/solvers/">The three solvers</a>
        <a href="/validation/#mms-tgv">The Taylor–Green record</a>
        <a href="/why/#algorithms">Why error attribution</a>
      </p>
    </section>
  </article>
</BaseLayout>

<style>
  .ppage { max-width: var(--w-panel); }
  .phead { margin-bottom: var(--space-7); }
  .phead h1 { margin: var(--space-3) 0 var(--space-4); }
  .phead .lede { color: var(--fg-1); max-width: var(--measure-lede); margin: 0; }

  .pgroup { margin-bottom: var(--space-7); }
  .pgroup h2 {
    font-size: var(--t-h3);
    padding-bottom: var(--space-3);
    border-bottom: 1px solid var(--line-1);
    margin-bottom: var(--space-4);
  }
  .body { color: var(--fg-1); font-size: var(--t-body-sm); max-width: var(--measure); margin: 0 0 var(--space-4); }

  table { width: 100%; border-collapse: collapse; font-size: var(--t-body-sm); margin-bottom: var(--space-4); }
  th, td { text-align: left; padding: var(--space-3); border-bottom: 1px solid var(--line-1); }
  thead th {
    font-family: var(--font-mono);
    font-size: var(--t-eyebrow);
    letter-spacing: var(--ls-eyebrow);
    text-transform: uppercase;
    color: var(--fg-2);
    font-weight: 400;
    white-space: nowrap;
  }
  tbody th { font-weight: 400; }
  tbody td { color: var(--fg-1); }
  tbody tr:last-child th, tbody tr:last-child td { border-bottom: none; }

  .more {
    display: flex;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    gap: var(--space-4);
    margin: 0;
    font-size: var(--t-body-sm);
  }
</style>
